Things this project would like to be better. Not issues -- issues are blueprints
for building something. These are wishes, and some of them may never become
anything.

--

I would like the frontline to be beautiful to watch. Not merely legible. A wave
meeting a wave should look like weather, and a wave meeting a wave that is
carrying three upgrades should look like weather losing an argument.

I would like a player who has never been told the rules to work out the command
radius from a single refusal message, and to feel clever about it rather than
annoyed. (This wish originally named the no-repeat-lane rule, which was cut as
arbitrary. The wish outlived the rule, which is a good sign about the wish.)

I would like the two economies to stay separate forever. Every game like this
eventually grows an exchange rate between its currencies, and the moment it does,
one of them becomes the real one and the other becomes a formality.

This one is already lost, and it took about a day. The reroll is that exchange
rate -- resource buys a place in the deck. It is narrow, it runs one way, and it
was argued for well, and none of that changes the fact that the sentence above
predicted exactly what happened and then it happened anyway. What is left of the
wish is smaller and worth keeping: **let there be exactly one exchange, let it
stay one-directional, and notice the day somebody proposes a second.**

I would like locking an upgrade to feel like putting a hand on somebody's arm.

I would like the library to mean something. It is the building you are defending
and it is called a library, and nobody has decided yet what that implies about
the world. It could imply a great deal.

I would like a match to be describable afterwards as a story with three acts,
because it has three surges in it, and a structure that arrives three times is a
structure a person can narrate.

I would like the terminal viewer to still be useful in five years.
